[{:seon.task/name "a-streamed-reply-test-sleeps-not"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: `seon.model-test/a-streamed-reply-ends-when-the-server-ends-it` writes its seven chunks with `(Thread/sleep 200)` between them (test/seon/model_test.clj:31), 1.4 s of a clock in a test (AGENTS.md section 9; notes/test-time-2026-10-23.md #12); `bin/laws clocks` names it (docs/clocks.md, owed its fix) since the clocks step reads test/. Correct: write the chunks without the sleep, each `>!!` its own write, so the test proves the reply ends when the server ends it, not when time passes."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.model-test/a-streamed-reply-ends-when-the-server-ends-it}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-model-call-follows-its-config"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: `seon.config/provider` resolves base URL, key variable and max-tokens through `seon.config/resolve-in` but reads `:seon.config.ai/thinking` and `:seon.config.ai/reasoning-effort` straight from the config it is given (src/seon/config.clj:267-268), so a model or a task cannot set them; root builds one provider at start and every call uses it; `:seon.task/config` admits only `:seon.config.agent/max-turns`. Correct (one resolver; v1 built each model request from its model's config): each request's settings (model, max-tokens, thinking, reasoning effort) come from `seon.config/resolve` for that request's task and model (task -> model -> provider -> config), `seon.model` builds the request from what it is handed and holds no defaults of its own, and a task's config may state the `:seon.config.ai/*` keys its model accepts. Extend the resolver and the request builder; no second path."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.model-test/a-request-carries-its-tasks-resolved-settings}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-reply-records-its-cached-tokens"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists (merged): `seon.model/reply-of` carries :cache-hit-tokens (DeepSeek's `prompt_cache_hit_tokens` or the OpenAI-compatible `prompt_tokens_details.cached_tokens`) and :cache-miss-tokens (`prompt_cache_miss_tokens`) when the provider reports them, absent when not (absent is not 0); the turn records `:seon.turn/cache-hit-tokens` and `:seon.turn/cache-miss-tokens` beside `:seon.turn/tokens`; the attempt's cache share is derived from its turns, never stored. Why: one attempt took 13 calls and 4,123,353 tokens (each turn resends the conversation under `:seon.config.render/history-budget` 256000), and DeepSeek bills a cached prefix at about 2% of a miss, so without the split its cost was anywhere from $0.01 to $0.58. Next: set the history budget from turns-to-green against cost."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.model-test/a-reply-reports-its-cache-hit-and-miss-tokens
                      seon.model-test/a-turn-records-what-the-provider-reported}}
 {:seon.task/name "budgets-are-data-and-spend-is-measured"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists (merged): unknown spend is absent, never 0. `seon.reconcile/judge-tx` takes turns, model-calls and tokens only from the end the agent wrote, and an attempt with no end is outcome :no-end with those counts absent; `seon.task/end-members` writes only the counts it was given; `seon.model/parse-reply` leaves out a count the provider did not send, and the scripted model's counts (`seon.print.select/estimate`) are marked :estimated; `seon.fx/model` states :cost only when both counts are known and `seon.fx/settle` charges nothing unmeasured (the bucket keeps its reservation); `seon.turn`'s act! sums a turn's tokens only when known. Left: `seon.e2e`'s summaries still read an absent count as 0 (`(:seon.attempt/model-calls % 0)`, src/seon/e2e.clj:72, :91)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.reconcile-test/an-attempt-with-no-end-records-no-fake-spend
                      seon.model-test/a-reply-without-usage-records-no-tokens}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-reply-is-bounded-by-what-a-reply-is"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: `:seon.config.ai/max-tokens` 65,536 in default.edn, ported from v1 where thinking was on (4,096 and 8,192 starved reasoning); thinking is now off by default. Reproduction (DeepSeek runs, thinking off): 66 of 370 turns ended finish length at 184k-415k characters, repetition loops whose every form was evaluated, spending 45% of all tokens and 168 of 181 model-minutes; the largest ordinary reply was 19,809 characters (about 5k tokens). Cause: the bound was set for reasoning, and `seon.model/request-body` sends it whether thinking is on or off. Correct: with thinking off the bound is the measured largest ordinary reply with headroom (about 8,192), its provenance in default.edn; a model or task with thinking on states its own through the resolver."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.model-test/a-request-with-thinking-off-carries-the-reply-bound}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-transport-failure-says-what-happens"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: `seon.model/transport-error`'s docstring says the request 'is kept and retried after a pause', but the :model owner (`seon.fx/owner-proc`) re-queues only :seon.model/rate-limited; a :seon.model/transport outcome is answered to the agent, whose attempt ends :error (docs/model.md#failures says so). AGENTS §4 allows a delayed insert only when the provider says retry. Correct: the docstring states what happens. Whether a dropped connection is retried once, as a provider-stated wait, is the owner's question; until then nothing retries it."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.model-test/a-transport-failure-is-answered-to-the-agent}}]
